WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.494
Imagine you're waiting in a long line for a concert.

00:00:03.494 --> 00:00:08.100
The first 100 people get a free T-shirt, so you want to know your position.

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:10.726
But you can't see the front of the line.

00:00:10.726 --> 00:00:13.570
And if you step out to count, you lose your place.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.680
So what can you do?

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:17.402
Here's one way.

00:00:17.402 --> 00:00:21.310
Everyone in the line follows the same simple rule.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:26.335
If you're at the front, you know your position: you're number one.

00:00:26.335 --> 00:00:31.860
Otherwise, ask the person in front of you for their number, and add one to it.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:39.438
The question travels forward, person by person, until it reaches the front.

00:00:39.438 --> 00:00:42.137
Then the answers travel back.

00:00:42.137 --> 00:00:45.300
1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6. You're number 6.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.302
This idea is called recursion.

00:00:48.302 --> 00:00:52.340
Welcome to Lecture 6 of Introduction to AI Programming.

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:56.529
In this first part, we'll see what a recursive function is,

00:00:56.529 --> 00:00:59.977
what Python really does when a function calls itself,

00:00:59.977 --> 00:01:02.970
and the three bugs that catch almost everyone.

00:01:03.767 --> 00:01:07.807
Recursion is when a function calls itself while it's running.

00:01:08.157 --> 00:01:10.801
Look again at the rule from the concert line.

00:01:10.801 --> 00:01:14.797
It has three parts, and every recursive function has the same three.

00:01:15.147 --> 00:01:20.680
First, a base case: the smallest problem, which you can answer directly.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:23.907
If you're at the front, you're number one.

00:01:24.507 --> 00:01:30.438
Second, a smaller problem of the same kind, solved by a recursive call.

00:01:30.438 --> 00:01:33.027
Ask the person in front of you.

00:01:33.627 --> 00:01:41.208
Third, a combine step, which turns the smaller answer into the answer for the whole problem.

00:01:41.208 --> 00:01:41.867
Add one.

00:01:42.467 --> 00:01:44.291
Keep these three parts in mind.

00:01:44.291 --> 00:01:46.467
We'll look for them in every example.

00:01:47.267 --> 00:01:50.646
Our first example is the factorial.

00:01:50.646 --> 00:01:56.728
5 factorial is 5 times 4 times 3 times 2 times 1, which is 120.

00:01:56.728 --> 00:02:00.107
And 0 factorial is defined to be 1.

00:02:00.457 --> 00:02:02.350
Now look closely.

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:06.137
What is 4 times 3 times 2 times 1?

00:02:06.687 --> 00:02:08.320
It's 4 factorial.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:11.971
So 5 factorial is 5 times 4 factorial.

00:02:11.971 --> 00:02:16.487
The factorial of 5 contains the factorial of 4.

00:02:16.837 --> 00:02:22.126
In general, n factorial is n times the factorial of n minus 1,

00:02:22.126 --> 00:02:23.918
and 0 factorial is 1.

00:02:23.918 --> 00:02:26.477
That's a recursive definition.

00:02:26.827 --> 00:02:30.227
And it turns into Python almost word for word.

00:02:30.577 --> 00:02:32.671
If n is 0, return 1.

00:02:32.671 --> 00:02:34.869
That's the base case.

00:02:34.869 --> 00:02:39.475
Otherwise, return n times fact of n minus 1.

00:02:39.475 --> 00:02:44.605
Calling fact on n minus 1 is the smaller problem,

00:02:44.605 --> 00:02:48.897
and multiplying by n is the combine step.

00:02:49.667 --> 00:02:53.157
Before we go on, pause the video and predict.

00:02:53.157 --> 00:02:55.252
What does fact of 3 return?

00:02:55.252 --> 00:02:58.587
And how many times is fact called in total?

00:02:59.137 --> 00:03:01.266
Fact of 3 returns 6.

00:03:01.266 --> 00:03:06.377
And fact is called 4 times: with 3, 2, 1, and 0.

00:03:07.167 --> 00:03:10.987
So what does Python actually do when fact calls fact?

00:03:10.987 --> 00:03:14.807
This is the picture you need to understand recursion.

00:03:15.157 --> 00:03:19.095
Every time a function is called, Python creates a new frame.

00:03:19.095 --> 00:03:22.837
A frame holds that call's parameters and local variables.

00:03:23.187 --> 00:03:24.841
We call fact of 3.

00:03:24.841 --> 00:03:27.964
Python makes a frame where n is 3.

00:03:27.964 --> 00:03:31.914
Since n isn't 0, this call needs fact of 2.

00:03:31.914 --> 00:03:34.027
So it stops, and waits.

00:03:34.377 --> 00:03:40.357
Fact of 2 gets its own frame, with its own n, equal to 2.

00:03:40.357 --> 00:03:42.770
It waits for fact of 1.

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:46.337
And fact of 1 waits for fact of 0.

00:03:46.687 --> 00:03:51.593
Look at the stack now: 4 frames, 4 different values of n,

00:03:51.593 --> 00:03:53.917
all alive at the same time.

00:03:53.917 --> 00:03:56.327
Only the top one is running.

00:03:56.927 --> 00:04:00.259
Fact of 0 hits the base case and returns 1.

00:04:00.259 --> 00:04:01.887
Its frame is removed.

00:04:02.237 --> 00:04:06.775
Now fact of 1 can finish: 1 times 1 is 1.

00:04:06.775 --> 00:04:10.206
Then fact of 2: 2 times 1 is 2.

00:04:10.206 --> 00:04:13.637
Then fact of 3: 3 times 2 is 6.

00:04:13.987 --> 00:04:18.972
The frames of unfinished calls form the call stack.

00:04:18.972 --> 00:04:22.197
A new call pushes a frame on top.

00:04:22.197 --> 00:04:24.250
A return pops it off.

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:26.107
Last in, first out.

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:30.700
Here's the most common misunderstanding about recursion.

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:36.676
Many students picture a recursive call as jumping back to the top of the function, like a loop,

00:04:36.676 --> 00:04:38.570
with n changed to the new value.

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:40.693
That's not what happens.

00:04:40.693 --> 00:04:45.197
A recursive call creates a fresh frame, with fresh variables.

00:04:45.197 --> 00:04:48.077
The caller's variable n doesn't change.

00:04:48.077 --> 00:04:55.240
When the call returns, the caller carries on from the middle of its own line, where n is still 3.

00:04:56.034 --> 00:04:58.292
You can watch the stack yourself.

00:04:58.292 --> 00:05:03.015
This version prints a line when each call starts and when it returns,

00:05:03.015 --> 00:05:05.274
indented by how deep the call is.

00:05:05.624 --> 00:05:12.864
First the calls go down: 3, then 2, then 1, then 0.

00:05:13.214 --> 00:05:16.570
Then the returns come back up, in the opposite order.

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:20.054
The first call to return is the last one that was made.

00:05:20.404 --> 00:05:25.644
You can also step through it in Python Tutor, and watch the frames appear and disappear.

00:05:26.434 --> 00:05:29.834
Tracing is the right way to check a recursive function.

00:05:29.834 --> 00:05:31.874
But it's a poor way to write one.

00:05:32.224 --> 00:05:37.907
When you write the recursive line, don't follow the call all the way down.

00:05:37.907 --> 00:05:39.289
Instead, trust it.

00:05:39.289 --> 00:05:43.744
Assume fact of n minus 1 already returns the right answer.

00:05:44.344 --> 00:05:46.808
Then ask just one question.

00:05:46.808 --> 00:05:51.464
If it does, is n times that the right answer for n?

00:05:52.014 --> 00:05:52.421
It is.

00:05:52.421 --> 00:05:59.012
So as long as the base case is right, and every call moves closer to it, the function is correct.

00:05:59.012 --> 00:06:01.934
This is called the recursive leap of faith.

00:06:02.701 --> 00:06:06.661
Finally, the three bugs that catch almost everyone.

00:06:07.011 --> 00:06:13.918
The first bug: no base case, or a base case that some inputs never reach.

00:06:13.918 --> 00:06:15.431
Call fact of -1.

00:06:15.431 --> 00:06:18.175
It calls fact of -2, then -3.

00:06:18.175 --> 00:06:21.771
The numbers move away from 0, forever.

00:06:22.121 --> 00:06:25.392
Python limits the stack to about 1,000 frames.

00:06:25.392 --> 00:06:28.521
Go past that, and you get a recursion error.

00:06:28.871 --> 00:06:31.056
The second bug: no progress.

00:06:31.056 --> 00:06:38.315
If the recursive call isn't on a smaller problem, say fact of n instead of fact of n minus 1,

00:06:38.315 --> 00:06:40.111
the same thing happens.

00:06:40.461 --> 00:06:43.401
The third bug: forgetting to return.

00:06:43.401 --> 00:06:49.772
This line computes n times fact of n minus 1, and then throws the result away.

00:06:49.772 --> 00:06:52.141
So the function returns None.

00:06:52.491 --> 00:06:55.301
And the error shows up one level up,

00:06:55.301 --> 00:06:58.971
because Python can't multiply a number by None.

00:06:59.767 --> 00:07:01.007
Let's sum up.

00:07:01.357 --> 00:07:06.797
A recursive function has a base case, a smaller problem, and a combine step.

00:07:07.147 --> 00:07:09.321
Every call gets its own frame,

00:07:09.321 --> 00:07:13.307
and the frames of unfinished calls form the call stack.

00:07:13.657 --> 00:07:16.032
Design with the leap of faith.

00:07:16.032 --> 00:07:17.377
Check by tracing.

00:07:17.727 --> 00:07:19.220
Here's one to try.

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:24.528
Write a function that returns the sum of the digits of a number,

00:07:24.528 --> 00:07:27.183
so that sum digits of 472 is 13.

00:07:27.183 --> 00:07:29.007
What's your base case?

00:07:29.557 --> 00:07:32.501
In part two, we'll use recursion on lists,

00:07:32.501 --> 00:07:37.477
and meet one of the most useful algorithms in computing: binary search.

